<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Gardens of the Roman Empire</title><link>https://roman-gardens.github.io/test-drafts/place/achaea/aegina/</link><description>Recent content on Gardens of the Roman Empire</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><atom:link href="https://roman-gardens.github.io/test-drafts/place/achaea/aegina/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Temple Grove</title><link>https://roman-gardens.github.io/test-drafts/place/achaea/aegina/aegina_temple_grove/</link><pubDate>Thu, 16 Jul 2020 09:45:49 -0400</pubDate><guid>https://roman-gardens.github.io/test-drafts/place/achaea/aegina/aegina_temple_grove/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="province">Province&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>&lt;a href="https://roman-gardens.github.io/test-drafts/place/achaea/">Achaea&lt;/a>&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="garden">Garden&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>Grove at the shrine of Aiakos&lt;/p>
&lt;h3 id="keywords">Keywords&lt;/h3>
&lt;ul>
&lt;li>&lt;a href="#">Literary&lt;/a>&lt;/li>
&lt;li>&lt;a href="http://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/610675-1">olive trees (&lt;em>Olea europaea&lt;/em>)&lt;/a>&lt;/li>
&lt;li>&lt;a href="http://vocab.getty.edu/page/aat/300251876">sacred groves&lt;/a>&lt;/li>
&lt;/ul>
&lt;h3 id="garden-description">Garden Description&lt;/h3>
&lt;p>Evidence for a temple grove on Aegina exists only in a literary reference. Pausanias &lt;a href="http://data.perseus.org/citations/urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0525.tlg001.perseus-eng1:2.29.6">(2.29.6)&lt;/a> wrote that the shrine of Aiakos was surrounded by a grove of olive trees. Pausanias says that it had been there since ancient times, and a reference in 464 B.C. to the grove in Pindar’s &lt;em>Olympian Odes&lt;/em> &lt;a href="http://data.perseus.org/citations/urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0033.tlg001.perseus-eng1:13">(13.109)&lt;/a> confirms that it was much older. This sanctuary was located in a prominent spot in the harbor town and was enclosed within a wall built of white stone. At the time of Pausanias’ travels in Greece in the 2nd century A.D., the temple grove was still to be seen.&lt;/p>
